            "text": "{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"domain\",\n      \"location\": \"Icc (0 : ℝ) 1\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The closed interval counts both endpoints of a period. For N = 1 and f(t) = sin(2πt), the zeroth derivative has three zeros in [0,1], while the first derivative has two. Counting multiplicities therefore contradicts the claimed monotonicity.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"The negation of the formal proposition is provable using this example, with mult chosen as the least nonzero derivative order at each point.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"negated_statement\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": null,\n      \"edit_from\": null,\n      \"edit_to\": null,\n      \"bridge\": \"translated\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Confirm that the informal zero count is intended to cover one period without counting its shared endpoint twice; the informal statement does not specify a counting interval.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"domain\",\n      \"location\": \"Icc (0 : ℝ) 1\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Replacing the closed counting interval by a half-open period would admit the sine example with a zero count different from the one required by the source hM hypothesis.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"There is an instance satisfying hM with Ico 0 1 but not hM with Icc 0 1, already at k = 0 for f(t) = sin(2πt).\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"hypothesis_separation\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": null,\n      \"edit_from\": \"Icc (0 : ℝ) 1\",\n      \"edit_to\": \"Ico (0 : ℝ) 1\",\n      \"bridge\": \"translated\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Verify that a half-open period, rather than another convention for counting zeros, is the intended interpretation. This probe distinguishes the hypotheses; it does not by itself establish the corrected theorem.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The closed interval in hM appears to double-count a periodic endpoint, making the formal monotonicity claim fail for a basic sine wave.\"\n}"
